TERMS AND CONDITIONS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

1. DEFINITIONS

   "Software" means the Meridian source code, binaries, documentation,
   and all associated files distributed under this license.

   "Protection Period" means six (6) years from the date each version
   of the Software is first publicly distributed. Each version carries
   its own independent Protection Period clock from its own release date.

   "Local Use" means installation and operation of the Software directly
   on the physical or virtual machine of each individual user, such that
   each user runs their own instance. Remote access to a centrally hosted
   instance does not constitute Local Use, regardless of whether such
   access is within a private network or organization.

   "Internal Self-Hosting" means deploying the Software on infrastructure
   owned or controlled by you or your organization, accessible only to
   members of that organization, and where no deployment knowledge,
   instructions, tooling, or guidance is published or shared outside
   that organization.

   "Competing Use" means any of the following:
   a) Making the Software or any substantially similar derivative
      available to third parties as a hosted, managed, or cloud-based
      service, whether for a fee or free of charge.
   b) Publishing, distributing, or sharing deployment instructions,
      Docker images, Helm charts, docker-compose files, or any tooling
      whose primary purpose is enabling others to host the Software
      or a derivative thereof.
   c) Building any product whose primary purpose substantially
      replicates or replaces Meridian's commercial offerings.

   "Hosted Database" means an external database service (such as
   PostgreSQL on Neon, Railway, Supabase, or similar providers) used
   as the data backend for locally-running instances of the Software.
   A Hosted Database alone does not constitute hosting the Software.

2. USE GRANT (FREE)

   During the Protection Period, permission is granted, free of charge,
   to any person or organization to:

   a) Use, copy, modify, and run the Software under Local Use for any
      purpose, personal or commercial, regardless of team size. There
      is no seat limit on Local Use.

   b) Connect locally-running instances to a shared Hosted Database
      for team coordination purposes. Using a Hosted Database does not
      require a commercial license regardless of team size.

   c) Self-host the Software under Internal Self-Hosting, provided
      such deployment remains strictly internal and no deployment
      knowledge, tooling, or instructions are published or shared
      outside the organization.

   d) Run the Software in local or self-owned CI/CD pipelines and
      automated workflows for your own projects.

   e) Distribute unmodified copies of the Software for non-commercial
      purposes, provided this license is retained in full.

   f) Use the Software for academic, research, and educational purposes.

3. RESTRICTIONS DURING PROTECTION PERIOD

   The following are NOT permitted without a separate written commercial
   license from the copyright holder:

   a) Competing Use as defined in Section 1, including but not limited
      to: offering Meridian as a hosted service, publishing deployment
      tooling or guides, or building competing products.

   b) Redistribution for commercial gain: selling, sublicensing, or
      distributing the Software or derivatives as part of any commercial
      product or service offering intended for third parties.

   c) Removing, altering, or obscuring this license or any copyright
      notices in the Software.

4. WHAT IS EXPLICITLY NOT RESTRICTED

   For the avoidance of doubt, the following are permitted and are
   NOT restricted by Section 3:

   a) Any number of developers each running Meridian locally on their
      own machines, even within the same organization.

   b) A team of any size where each member installs and runs the
      Software locally, all pointing at a shared Hosted Database
      (e.g. Postgres on Neon or Supabase) for coordination.

   c) An organization deploying Meridian on internal infrastructure
      for internal use only, provided they do not publish or share
      deployment tooling or instructions externally.

   d) Forking and modifying the Software for internal use, provided
      modifications are not published as a competing product or
      hosting solution.

   e) A solo developer using Meridian to coordinate AI agents across
      their own sessions, projects, and CI/CD pipelines.

